WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.100
- I'm X.

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.200
- I'm Diane Warren.

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:03.915
- And this is the Academy Green Room.

00:00:03.915 --> 00:00:06.498
(upbeat music)

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.530
Hey, X is back

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.738
and I'm here with the goat.

00:00:12.738 --> 00:00:14.119
The goat of songwriting.

00:00:14.119 --> 00:00:14.952
- Goat.

00:00:14.952 --> 00:00:15.785
- The GOAT.
- There's a goat here?

00:00:15.785 --> 00:00:17.180
- The Greatest Of All Time.

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:18.307
Yes, there is a GOAT in the building.

00:00:18.307 --> 00:00:20.030
- I have a rescue ranch.

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:21.317
- Do you do?

00:00:21.317 --> 00:00:22.150
- I have a farm animal rescue ranch.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:23.410
And there's real goats there.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:24.670
- Well...
- Those are the real goats.

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:26.220
- No, you're the real GOAT.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.024
And then you also have goats.

00:00:28.024 --> 00:00:30.087
You're the greatest when
it comes to songwriting.

00:00:30.087 --> 00:00:31.223
- I know I'm not.

00:00:31.223 --> 00:00:32.220
- But I'm claiming it on your behalf.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:33.053
- Thank you, but I'm not.

00:00:33.053 --> 00:00:34.090
- Diane, I have,

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:37.838
and see that's why you
that's what the greats do.

00:00:37.838 --> 00:00:39.730
You're humble.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:41.920
You're humble in your greatness.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:43.210
And I'm gonna claim it for you.

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:44.043
- Okay.

00:00:44.043 --> 00:00:45.060
- Now, normally when I do interviews,

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.460
I don't have access right now.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:48.620
I would have an air horn on my intro.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:49.980
And I'm gonna give you
a big intro right now.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.430
'Cause we're on Spotify Live.

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:53.850
There's an audience
tapped in right now live,

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:55.140
we're in the Academy Green Room.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:56.567
We're on Green Room.

00:00:56.567 --> 00:01:00.172
13 Oscar nominations and counting.

00:01:00.172 --> 00:01:01.005
- Yeah.

00:01:01.005 --> 00:01:02.860
- You said something to me
right before we started.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:05.760
You were like, that means I lost 12 times.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.360
- Yeah, but I was nominated 12 times.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:08.473
- That's what I'm saying.

00:01:08.473 --> 00:01:09.450
- Actually nominated 13 times.

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:10.940
It's a big deal to be nominated.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:11.773
- I think so too.

00:01:11.773 --> 00:01:14.640
- Five songs are picked, only five songs.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.529
Grammy's have how many song categories?

00:01:16.529 --> 00:01:17.533
- That's right.

00:01:17.533 --> 00:01:18.366
- Like endless,

00:01:18.366 --> 00:01:20.936
and they have 40 song categories.

00:01:20.936 --> 00:01:24.092
You know the Oscars have one, five songs.

00:01:24.092 --> 00:01:24.925
- That's right.

00:01:24.925 --> 00:01:26.910
- Think of all the songs released all year

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:28.250
and all the movies.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:29.150
They only pick five.

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:31.811
If I'm one of those, that's winning.

00:01:31.811 --> 00:01:33.843
- Let me, I'm gonna jump right in it.

00:01:33.843 --> 00:01:38.010
How did writing for film
become a specialty of yours?

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:39.790
You clearly have a knack for it.

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:40.733
- I love doing it.

00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:42.680
I mean, I just love writing songs

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.818
and it's fun to kind of
capture what a movie,

00:01:45.818 --> 00:01:47.450
the heart of a movie.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:48.283
- Yeah.

00:01:48.283 --> 00:01:49.116
- That's what I like to do.

00:01:49.116 --> 00:01:50.370
- What is your process?

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:51.930
- I process everything.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:52.763
Yeah, okay.

00:01:52.763 --> 00:01:54.103
My process, yeah,

00:01:54.103 --> 00:01:56.410
in general writing is I show up

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:57.243
and do the work,

00:01:57.243 --> 00:02:01.120
but for movies, I either read a script,

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.706
or preferably I see it.

00:02:03.706 --> 00:02:06.810
I see a rough cut of it, which
like with four good days,

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:08.890
the one with somehow you do

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:10.437
that I'm nominated for this year.

00:02:10.437 --> 00:02:11.380
- MacIntyre.

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:13.200
- MacIntyre will be singing it Sunday,

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.553
which I can't wait.

00:02:14.553 --> 00:02:15.386
- Which is amazing.

00:02:15.386 --> 00:02:16.250
- I hear it's gonna be amazing.

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:17.083
- I'm sure it is.

00:02:17.083 --> 00:02:17.916
- Yeah.

00:02:17.916 --> 00:02:18.749
- She's a legend.

00:02:18.749 --> 00:02:19.582
- Yeah, she's great.

00:02:19.582 --> 00:02:21.550
And she was perfect for this song

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:22.920
and perfect for this movie.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.041
And when I do a song
for a movie I'm casting

00:02:25.041 --> 00:02:26.864
the artist for the song,

00:02:26.864 --> 00:02:29.310
that has to be authentic to the song

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:30.610
and authentic to the movie.

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:31.620
- Right.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:32.453
- And she was both.

00:02:32.453 --> 00:02:34.105
- How do you know when you nailed it?

00:02:34.105 --> 00:02:34.938
Like you--

00:02:34.938 --> 00:02:36.380
- I feel it.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:38.386
It's just like, I'm
really a perfectionist.

00:02:38.386 --> 00:02:43.386
And so when I'm satisfied
with something, I'm usually--

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.587
- How many hours would you say you spend

00:02:46.587 --> 00:02:47.589
on one of those songs?

00:02:47.589 --> 00:02:48.837
- Oh, I could spend a week on a song,

00:02:48.837 --> 00:02:51.858
but lyrics are what I
take really time with,

00:02:51.858 --> 00:02:56.858
that's the hard part, but
I'm really meticulous,

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:01.153
with lyrics

00:03:01.153 --> 00:03:01.986
and music too,

00:03:01.986 --> 00:03:04.228
but the music comes a little more easy,

00:03:04.228 --> 00:03:06.767
but now then I gotta kind of
figure out what the story is,

00:03:06.767 --> 00:03:08.730
then I got a really great chorus,

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.197
but how do I set that up?

00:03:10.197 --> 00:03:11.750
I have a great first verse,

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:13.170
but then where do I go?

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:14.003
And then, okay.

00:03:14.003 --> 00:03:15.702
I have a great second verse then

00:03:15.702 --> 00:03:16.810
where do I do with that bridge?

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:19.310
How do I, how does that,
take it to the next level

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:21.900
it takes work.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:23.670
- How much time do you spend with the film

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:25.010
before you even start writing?

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:25.843
Or you just start?

00:03:25.843 --> 00:03:27.520
- I just see it or I read the script.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:28.713
- Just once.

00:03:28.713 --> 00:03:29.546
- Yeah.

00:03:31.745 --> 00:03:33.530
- And then what are the conversations

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:35.810
like with the directors?

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:38.950
Because one thing about film
is the collaborative process.

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:40.593
- Usually they trust me.

00:03:42.441 --> 00:03:47.000
Like again, I'll, with
this song, I had the chorus

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:48.750
and I played it for the director,

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:50.680
I had piece of verse too,

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:52.225
and he loved it.

00:03:52.225 --> 00:03:54.537
So that was good.

00:03:54.537 --> 00:03:56.802
- You have done so many--

00:03:56.802 --> 00:03:58.120
- Better them when they hate it,

00:03:58.120 --> 00:03:59.020
but that doesn't usually happen.

00:03:59.020 --> 00:03:59.853
- I was about to say--

00:03:59.853 --> 00:04:02.090
- I'm at the point where I play something,

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:03.520
I'm really excited about it.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:05.180
I won't play for somebody
unless I love it.

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:07.060
- Tell me about a time where you loved it

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:09.400
and you were shocked
that they didn't love it

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:10.600
as much as you did

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:11.433
or did it happen?

00:04:11.433 --> 00:04:14.330
- Well, there's a couple of,

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:17.702
but you know what, but
there's more that works out

00:04:17.702 --> 00:04:19.583
than that don't to be honest.

00:04:19.583 --> 00:04:21.070
- But how do you take it?

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:22.860
Because you've had so much success

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:24.487
and I think people can learn from it.

00:04:24.487 --> 00:04:27.084
That's the reason I'm
pressing a little bit.

00:04:27.084 --> 00:04:29.702
- Sometimes they don't
take a song for a movie

00:04:29.702 --> 00:04:32.280
and then sometimes
something else happens with.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:34.180
I remember writing something for,

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:36.600
I think it was "Sisterhood
of the Traveling Pants"

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:38.197
I wrote a really great song for it

00:04:38.197 --> 00:04:39.370
and they didn't use it.

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:40.440
I was kind of like bummed out,

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:43.520
but right then Carrie
Underwood was doing an album

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:45.010
and she loved that song

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:46.293
and she did it.

00:04:46.293 --> 00:04:47.470
And that album sold 12 million albums

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:48.790
and that worked out,

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:49.623
- It found another line.

00:04:49.623 --> 00:04:51.273
- And if it was in that movie there,

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:52.943
I don't know who the artist was gonna be

00:04:52.943 --> 00:04:54.363
or anything.

00:04:54.363 --> 00:04:55.300
And so, you know what,
that they did me a favor.

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:57.950
- Already got a question
from the audience.

00:04:57.950 --> 00:04:58.980
Diane is a legend

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:01.700
and has worked with so many iconic people.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:02.980
It's gonna be a tough question for you,

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:04.580
who has been your favorite?

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:05.552
And they said, hi, Diane.

00:05:05.552 --> 00:05:07.240
- Oh, hi, what's your name?

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:08.717
- Hi, whatever your name is.

00:05:08.717 --> 00:05:10.570
- Let's get the name up.

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:11.666
- They're all my favorites.

00:05:11.666 --> 00:05:13.280
Is that a great question?

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:14.330
- But I think that's favorite.

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:15.280
- I mean, a great answer?

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:17.495
A great, like, not get
myself in trouble answer?

00:05:17.495 --> 00:05:19.060
- I would've answered the same way.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:19.893
- Okay.

00:05:19.893 --> 00:05:20.726
- Like, I mean, what are you gonna say?

00:05:20.726 --> 00:05:22.292
- Yeah, if I say someone's my favorite

00:05:22.292 --> 00:05:23.790
and I go wait, that's okay.

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:25.633
- But what about, what's your favorite,

00:05:25.633 --> 00:05:26.923
I thought we really had something.

00:05:26.923 --> 00:05:27.940
- Yeah, what's up with that, like, yeah.

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:28.940
I'm not gonna work with you again,

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:30.580
cause I'm not your favorite.

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:32.480
- I mean, it's so much I can ask you.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:35.429
I mean, so many songs,
like nine number one songs

00:05:35.429 --> 00:05:37.170
on the Billboard,

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:41.360
32 top 10 songs on a Billboard Hot 100,

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:44.170
including "If I could
turn back time", "Share".

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:46.426
I wanna know some of the
stories of these songs.

00:05:46.426 --> 00:05:50.767
And does it ever get to a
point where you make a song

00:05:50.767 --> 00:05:53.013
that's so big that you get tired of it?

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:55.550
- No, I don't get tired of it.

00:05:55.550 --> 00:05:57.380
'Cause if I'm getting tired
of it, that's a good thing.

00:05:57.380 --> 00:05:59.523
I mean, if I'm like, if it's
getting it's a play too much.

00:05:59.523 --> 00:06:00.795
- It's like a good problem.

00:06:00.795 --> 00:06:01.628
- That's what I mean, yeah.

00:06:01.628 --> 00:06:02.461
- It's like, I don't, know,

00:06:02.461 --> 00:06:04.154
I'm grateful for any success

00:06:04.154 --> 00:06:06.369
or if people know my songs.

00:06:06.369 --> 00:06:07.202
- Yeah.

00:06:07.202 --> 00:06:09.320
- Or like right now on TikTok,

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:11.130
I wrote a song for Paloma Faith.

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:12.470
That was a number one record,

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:14.710
like outside of America, everywhere else.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:16.440
'Cause she, no one knows who she is here.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:17.273
Now all of a sudden on TikTok,

00:06:17.273 --> 00:06:21.350
it has like 350,000
videos in the last month

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:22.183
or something.

00:06:22.183 --> 00:06:23.240
So it's like, wow,

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:25.430
that's a whole other world
that I've never been a part of.

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:29.620
So that just gave a whole
other life to that song.

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:31.190
- How do you keep it fresh?

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:32.701
Like how do you get inspired.

00:06:32.701 --> 00:06:33.940
- I work every day.

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:35.642
I get inspired every day
I come up with ideas,

00:06:35.642 --> 00:06:37.846
I just, I work.

00:06:37.846 --> 00:06:39.373
- Well, what's the--

00:06:39.373 --> 00:06:41.575
- It's like an Olympic athlete.

00:06:41.575 --> 00:06:43.446
If you wanna be in the
Olympics, you better practice.

00:06:43.446 --> 00:06:45.891
And you have to practice every day

00:06:45.891 --> 00:06:46.905
and you have to work

00:06:46.905 --> 00:06:47.930
and you have to put in the time

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:48.805
and you have to put in the work.

00:06:48.805 --> 00:06:49.638
- Get a rep set.

00:06:49.638 --> 00:06:50.471
- It's that simple.

00:06:50.471 --> 00:06:51.730
- Do you study other artists?

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:53.100
Are there any other artists?

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:54.280
- No.
- That you just--

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:55.540
- I don't study artists,

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:58.080
but I of course been influenced by stuff.

00:06:58.080 --> 00:06:59.751
- Yeah, I wanna go back then,

00:06:59.751 --> 00:07:02.110
'cause I'm sitting now with a legend.

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:03.170
I really mean that.

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:04.480
I know you don't like that.

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:05.508
You're a legend Diane.

00:07:05.508 --> 00:07:06.520
- Makes me feel old.

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:08.005
- Well, it's not about that.

00:07:08.005 --> 00:07:09.580
It's just about your accomplishments.

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:12.370
You've been a legend for some time just

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:14.160
because you've had some
amazing accomplishments.

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:15.329
- I'm still here.

00:07:15.329 --> 00:07:16.162
I'm still in the game.

00:07:16.162 --> 00:07:16.995
- That's right.

00:07:16.995 --> 00:07:18.309
You're still acting.

00:07:18.309 --> 00:07:19.311
- I'm still kicking ass.

00:07:19.311 --> 00:07:20.337
- That's right.

00:07:20.337 --> 00:07:21.352
- Yeah.

00:07:21.352 --> 00:07:22.323
That's the kind of, what's awesome.

00:07:22.323 --> 00:07:24.518
Like if I had a choice,
like back to the Oscars,

00:07:24.518 --> 00:07:25.777
if I had a choice of winning

00:07:25.777 --> 00:07:27.759
and being one of these nominations.

00:07:27.759 --> 00:07:28.592
- Yeah.

00:07:28.592 --> 00:07:31.403
- And never being nominated
again, I would choose to,

00:07:32.282 --> 00:07:33.808
the longevity of 34 years

00:07:33.808 --> 00:07:36.110
and 13 nominations.

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:40.680
- Can we go back to when
you first wrote for a movie,

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:41.540
what was that story?

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:42.610
How did that come about?

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:46.649
- That was my first big hit
really was "Rhythm of the Night"

00:07:46.649 --> 00:07:47.685
- What a great--

00:07:47.685 --> 00:07:48.518
- DeBarge.

00:07:48.518 --> 00:07:49.351
- What a great song.

00:07:49.351 --> 00:07:50.435
- Thank you.

00:07:50.435 --> 00:07:51.268
- What a great song.

00:07:51.268 --> 00:07:52.270
- I love that song.

00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:53.103
So yeah,

00:07:54.026 --> 00:07:55.920
and it was what was
really awesome for me is

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:57.550
I'm such a Motown fan

00:07:57.550 --> 00:07:58.690
and growing up,

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:02.660
I just loved all every artist
on Motown, all the songs.

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:06.090
So for my first big hit to
be on Motown with Barry Gordy

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:07.880
it's a coolest thing ever, yeah.

00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:09.966
- And then from there,
was it just kind of like,

00:08:09.966 --> 00:08:10.799
then you got the next one,

00:08:10.799 --> 00:08:12.370
like somebody heard that, liked it

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:14.570
and then offered you the next one.

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:15.660
- You're talking about movies.

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:16.817
- Yeah.

00:08:16.817 --> 00:08:18.037
- Yeah, I just kind of kept doing it.

00:08:18.037 --> 00:08:20.128
Like, I just think it's something,

00:08:20.128 --> 00:08:24.068
that my dad put so as
long as I went to college,

00:08:24.068 --> 00:08:25.650
he'd support me.

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:27.650
And so I took all these
real like movie classes,

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:29.010
so I didn't have to do anything.

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:30.420
So it was basically a college rabbi.

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:31.944
So I took a lot of like film history.

00:08:31.944 --> 00:08:33.110
- Yeah.

00:08:33.110 --> 00:08:34.500
- Watched old movies and stuff like that.

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:38.833
But I think in doing that I almost maybe,

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:41.500
